        "_comment": "The browser journey each frontend app's playwright.config.ts reads (`make test-fe-e2e`): it builds the site, serves it with the stack's own preview server on http://<host>:<port> (an IPv6 host is written without brackets), and waits server_timeout_ms for it before the run fails. A port another process holds fails the run rather than reusing that server. browser_executable null uses the runner's own browser cache, located by PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH when set (child_env.names passes it to every make verify goal); a path launches that browser instead. Nothing here downloads a browser: `make fe-browsers` does, through each app's npm script test:e2e:browsers, and without one in the cache the journey fails.",
        "host": "127.0.0.1",
        "port": 4391,
        "server_timeout_ms": 120000,
        "browser_executable": null
      }
    },
{%- endif %}
    "backend": {
      "language": "python",
      "python": "{{ backend_python }}",
      "path": "src/backend"
    },
    "app": {
      "language": "python",
      "path": "src/app",
      "module": "app"
    }
  },
  "transports": {
    "enabled": ["rest", "mcp"{% for t in transports if t not in ["rest", "mcp"] %}, "{{ t }}"{% endfor %}],
    "available": {
      "rest": "api/rest_fastapi",
      "mcp": "mcp"{% if "grpc" in transports %},
      "grpc": "api/grpc"{% endif %}{% if "edge_nginx" in transports %},
      "edge_nginx": "api/edge_nginx"{% endif %}
    }
  },
  "e2e": {
    "_comment": "End-to-end coverage (CONVENTIONS section 17): every endpoint an enabled transport serves is named by a scenario under scenario_dir with @pytest.mark.<marker>(\"<transport> <id>\"), or exempt here with a reason. tests/integration/test_e2e_coverage.py derives the endpoints from the code, never from a list: sources maps a transport to how (openapi: the app's OpenAPI document, written by openapi_exporter inside the REST transport directory; mcp-stdio: each __main__-guarded server asked over its stdio, with mcp_env added to its environment; proto: the transport's .proto service methods; none: the transport serves no endpoint of its own). list_timeout_s bounds each child a derivation or a scenario starts: the walk over the routes the REST app serves, the exporter, and each MCP server. exempt maps an endpoint id to the reason it has no scenario; a stale, redundant or reasonless entry is red. server is how tests/live_server.py starts the REST app for a scenario: uvicorn serving app on host, ready when probe_path answers 200 within ready_timeout_s (polled every probe_interval_s), each request bounded by request_timeout_s, and stopped by SIGTERM then SIGKILL after stop_timeout_s. There is no port: the OS assigns a free one. make run-api's fixed port is a separate value.",
    "scenario_dir": "tests/e2e",
    "marker": "endpoint",
    "sources": {
{% if "edge_nginx" in transports %}      "edge_nginx": "none",
{% endif %}{% if "grpc" in transports %}      "grpc": "proto",
{% endif %}      "mcp": "mcp-stdio",
      "rest": "openapi"
    },
    "openapi_exporter": "export_openapi.py",
    "list_timeout_s": 30,
    "mcp_env": {
      "KEEL_MCP_MODEL": "fake"
    },
    "exempt": {% if "grpc" in transports %}{"grpc /thing.v1.ThingService/CreateThing": "No gate generates the gRPC stubs (make -C api/grpc gen) or installs grpcio, so no scenario can call this method. Add a scenario and delete this entry once they ship."}{% else %}{}{% endif %},
    "server": {
      "host": "127.0.0.1",
      "app": "app:app",
      "probe_path": "/health",
      "ready_timeout_s": 30,
      "probe_interval_s": 0.1,
      "request_timeout_s": 30,
      "stop_timeout_s": 10
    }
  },
  "tests": {
    "_comment": "Where test scratch lives, and what a test may reach outside itself, by tier. scratch_root is the directory every test's scratch goes under: tests/conftest.py reads it through scripts/config_loader.py (KEEL_TEST_SCRATCH wins over it), expands ~, and points tempfile, TMPDIR and pytest's numbered basetemp there before any test module is imported; pyproject.toml's tmp_path_retention_policy then removes a passed test's directory and keeps a failed one's, named after the test. It must be absolute, with no .. part, and neither under the system temporary directory, which may be memory-backed, nor inside the checkout, whose tree walkers would read a kept directory; anything else stops pytest before any test runs. A root that holds the checkout is accepted, and the checkout stays outside it. tests/isolation_guard.py reads this block through scripts/config_loader.py, and tests/conftest.py installs it as a Python audit hook that judges each test's setup, call and teardown. isolation.tiers maps a tier marker to its rule on three axes: network (none: no socket connect, sendto or sendmsg with an address, an AF_UNIX path inside a temp root aside; loopback: also 127.0.0.0/8, ::1 and the name localhost; any: anywhere), writes (temp: a file, directory or link is created, changed or removed, its mode, owner, flags, times or extended attributes changed, or an AF_UNIX socket bound to a path, only under scratch_root or pytest's basetemp; any: anywhere) and stores (temp: a sqlite database is opened only under those roots, a file: name judged both as a URI and as a file in the working directory, and a store listed in isolation.stores is never opened in any mode; any: anywhere). The closest tier marker on a test decides; a test with no marker that names a row here gets the strictest value on every axis. A reach outside the rule fails the test, named, even when the test catches the error. isolation.exempt.<axis> maps a test's node id to the reason it may reach outside its tier on that axis; a blank reason is refused before any test runs, and an exemption whose test ran and reached nothing on that axis fails the run. isolation.stores lists the live stores, relative to the project root or absolute; a test reaches one only through the store_copy fixture, which copies it with its -wal, -shm and -journal siblings into the test's own directory. Defence-in-depth, not a sandbox: a child process is not audited, and a name lookup is not judged, by choice. child_timeout_seconds is how long a test of the shipped harness lets a child Python or a nested pytest session run before it kills it and fails. docs/adr/keel/K-0024-what-a-test-may-reach.md is the decision.",
    "child_timeout_seconds": 600,
    "scratch_root": "~/.cache/{{ project_slug }}/tests",
    "isolation": {
      "tiers": {
        "unit": {"network": "none", "writes": "temp", "stores": "temp"},
        "integration": {"network": "loopback", "writes": "temp", "stores": "temp"},
        "e2e": {"network": "loopback", "writes": "temp", "stores": "temp"},
        "smoke": {"network": "any", "writes": "temp", "stores": "temp"}
      },
      "exempt": {
        "network": {},
        "writes": {},
        "stores": {}
      },
      "stores": []
    }
  },
  "traceability": {
    "_comment": "Requirement traceability (CONVENTIONS section 17): every requirement a `kind: spec` document under specs_root defines is named by a test under tests_root with @pytest.mark.<marker>(\"<id>\"), or excused in gaps with a reason. scripts/trace_requirements.py (make trace) judges it and writes the matrix to matrix_path, whose shape, version and length caps matrix_schema fixes; matrix_path must be git-ignored. id_prefixes is the closed registry of id prefixes: an id is <prefix> then exactly digits ASCII digits, optionally followed by #AC-<n> for one numbered criterion; the longest listed prefix wins and an unlisted prefix is refused. family requirement is what a test covers; family goal is recognised and never traced. source self means a spec here defines the ids; source spine means another system does, so a spec here may not. gaps maps a unit's id (a criterion, or a requirement that has none) to the reason no test names it; a gap for a covered, undefined or malformed id is red. no_requirements is null, or the reason this project has no requirement yet: with zero requirements and null the gate is red, and the reason is red once a requirement or a marker exists.",
    "id_prefixes": [
      {
        "prefix": "REQ-",
        "family": "requirement",
        "digits": 4,
        "source": "self"
      }
    ],
    "specs_root": "docs/specs",
    "tests_root": "tests",
    "marker": "req",
    "gaps": {},
    "no_requirements": null,
    "matrix_path": ".evidence/traceability/matrix.json",
    "matrix_schema": "config/traceability/matrix.schema.json"
  },
  "runtimes": {
    "_comment": "Agent control-flow engines this project makes available (registered in runtimes/registry.py, CONVENTIONS section 16). 'default' is declarative: check_H requires it to be in 'available' and each engine's directory to exist, but runtimes/registry.py DEFAULT_RUNTIME decides what get_runtime() returns with no name, and nothing ties the two together.",
    "default": "inprocess",
    "available": {
      "inprocess": "runtimes",
      "langgraph": "runtimes"
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "_comment": "Model/provider adapters this project makes available (registered in models/registry.py). 'default' is declarative: check_H requires it to be in 'available' and the showcase marks it, but models/registry.py DEFAULT_MODEL decides what get_model() returns with no name, and nothing ties the two together; the showcase surfaces this list. credential_env names, per adapter, the credential environment variables it may use: for an adapter that starts a process, what that process gets on top of child_env (scripts/child_env.py build_child_env(credentials_for=<adapter>)), an adapter absent from it getting the base allowlist only; for openai-compatible, which sends a credential itself, the only names its api_key_env argument accepts. Add a name here, not in code, when a CLI needs one more (for example a cloud provider's region). allowed_hosts names, per adapter that sends a credential itself, the `host` or `host:port` entries it may send it to, each in its one canonical form (scripts/child_env.py canonical_host_entry: a lower-case ASCII name, a dotted-quad IPv4 address or a bracketed compressed IPv6 address); `host` matches only the scheme's default port and nothing matches by suffix, prefix or wildcard. It ships empty, so openai-compatible refuses every base_url, its default included, until the project lists the endpoint's host; an adapter absent from it may send its credential nowhere. Defence in depth, not a sandbox (docs/adr/keel/K-0018-model-endpoint-host-allowlist.md).",
    "default": "claude-code-headless",
    "available": {
      "claude-code-headless": "models",
      "openai-compatible": "models",
      "fake": "models"
    },
    "allowed_hosts": {
      "openai-compatible": []
    },
    "credential_env": {
      "claude-code-headless": ["ANTHROPIC_API_KEY", "CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"],
      "openai-compatible": ["OPENAI_API_KEY"]
    }
  },
  "import_layers": {
    "_comment": "The layers a Python import may cross, bottom to top: a file may import a file in its own layer or a lower one, never a higher one, so everything flows up. A file sits in the layer of its longest prefix, matched by whole path segments. search_roots are the directories an import name is looked up from, in order, the first holding it winning: the repository root and the sys.path entries keel's entry points and tests add. The order comes from CONVENTIONS section 2 (the directories), src/AGENT.md (app -> {frontend, backend} -> shared) and the measured import graph: scripts/child_env.py and scripts/exit_codes.py, both stdlib-only, sit at the bottom because models, agents, mcp, evals and api import them, and scripts/config_loader.py, stdlib-only too, sits with them as the config reader any layer may import; tests/integration/test_gate_scope.py holds every file of the bottom layer to the oldest interpreter a hook runs, and the rest of scripts/ sits above api, mcp and demo because scripts/doc_review.py imports agents. tests/integration/test_import_layers_gate.py reads search_roots and order and fails an upward import, a file no prefix places, and a prefix on disk that places no file; a prefix whose path is absent, such as a transport this project did not select, is skipped. Place a new top-level directory that holds Python here. may_end_the_process maps a module another non-test module imports to the reason it still ends the process outside its entry block; tests/integration/test_exit_gate.py reads it, and an entry that no longer names such a module, or whose reason is blank, is red. docs/adr/keel/K-0021-imports-flow-up-a-configured-layer-order.md is the decision.",
    "search_roots": ["", "src/", "tests/", "scripts/", "scripts/jobs/", "scripts/hooks/", "scripts/agent_surface/", "mcp/", "api/rest_fastapi/", "tests/integration/"],
    "order": [
      ["scripts/child_env.py", "scripts/config_loader.py", "scripts/exit_codes.py"],
      ["src/shared"],
      ["src/backend"],
      ["src/app"],
      ["models", "runtimes"],
      ["agents"],
      ["evals"],
      ["api", "mcp", "demo"],
      ["scripts"],
      ["tests"]
    ],
    "may_end_the_process": {
      "scripts/jobs/conflict_guard.py": "exit_if_conflicted runs in a job's entry block before the job imports anything else of the project, so there is no main yet to return a member to, and the job's next import may be the conflicted file; it ends the process through exit_codes.fail, imported only at that moment"
    }
  },
  "config_reads": {
    "_comment": "A heuristic over the syntax tree: tests/integration/test_config_reads_gate.py walks the Python files under `roots`, scripts/config_loader.py left out, and fails a direct read of a file scripts/config_loader.py PROJECT_READS declares (an open, read_text or read_bytes of a path built from constants, a bound name, a starred split, another project module's path constant imported by name or read as an attribute, or a same-module helper handed one) and a direct read of a variable the config declares as carrying a value (environ, getenv), because a value read twice is read by two rules; an open for writing counts too. The loader is the one reader, so a default, a type and a secret are judged once. It has known false negatives: a path handed in as a parameter, an attribute of a module imported inside a function or relatively, a function of another module that opens what it is given, a method called through an object, a variable whose name the code holds in a variable, a read through subprocess or importlib, and a path built from a value that is not a constant. tests/ is not walked: it reads config as its own policy and to build fixtures. `allowed` maps each file that still reads directly to the reason; an entry whose reason is blank, whose file is absent or outside the roots, or whose file no longer reads directly is red, and the test's _CEILING caps the count, so the map only shrinks. Where the gate runs tells keel's files from a project's own: in the template repository, which holds copier.yml, every finding is red; a generated project carries no list of the files keel shipped, and keel's were judged in the template, so there the two finding tests skip and name each direct read they saw, until the warn-then-fail tier for a project's own code is built. docs/adr/keel/K-0022-one-config-loader.md is the decision.",
    "roots": ["agents", "api", "demo", "evals", "mcp", "models", "runtimes", "scripts", "src"],
    "allowed": {
      "agents/practice_refactor/_brain.py": "opens the practices registry under the corpus root its caller hands it, not this project's; it stays until a loader test covers a root an agent is handed",
{%- if false %}
      "scripts/audit_project.py": "opens the manifest of the generated project it audits, which is data under audit and not this project's configuration",
{%- endif %}
      "scripts/check_structure.py": "the gate validates the manifest's shape under the oldest host interpreter before any loader row can be trusted, at twelve sites, so moving it onto the loader is a change of its own",
      "scripts/jobs/declare_no_app.py": "rewrites the manifest's text in place, so it reads the file as text to edit, not as configuration",
{%- if showcase %}
      "scripts/jobs/export_showcase_static.py": "opens the manifest for layers.frontend inside a copier job; importing the loader would add config/practices.json to the files scripts/jobs/conflict_guard.py refuses the job over, and no test yet proves the job under that refusal",
{%- endif %}
      "scripts/jobs/restamp_docs.py": "reads the manifest's raw bytes to find a merge-conflict marker before anything parses it, not a configured value",
      "scripts/launch.py": "opens the manifest for the launcher block, whose one reader scripts/long_scripts.py imports; the launcher block has no KEYS rows yet, and the two modules move onto the loader together",
      "scripts/long_scripts.py": "opens the manifest for the launcher block through the reader scripts/launch.py owns, and moves onto the loader with scripts/launch.py",
      "scripts/run_app.py": "opens the manifest for layers.app, where absent and null are two refusals; that split needs a KEYS row and an exit-code decision not yet made",
{%- if showcase %}
      "src/backend/showcase/_repo.py": "opens the manifest and the practices registry for the showcase read model, whose keys have no KEYS rows yet",
{%- endif %}
      "scripts/trace_requirements.py": "opens the manifest for the traceability block, which has no KEYS rows yet",
      "src/backend/shared/__init__.py": "opens the manifest for the project name at import time; through the loader, a read at import would write a default line in every process that imports the package, and whether it should is not decided"
    }
  },
  "change_comments": {
    "_comment": "A heuristic over the comments that open a thought (tests/source_tree.py comment_starts, read through tokenize, so a string is never a comment): tests/integration/test_change_comment_gate.py fails one whose text, lowercased, starts with a phrase here followed by a character that is not a letter or digit, or by its end, because such a comment reports a change instead of explaining the code (docs/guides/python-style.md section 2). It has known false negatives: a phrase in mid-sentence, and a change reported in other words. A comment opening with one of `directives` is an instruction to a tool (a shebang, a type, lint or coverage pragma, a formatter switch), not prose, and is skipped. Add a phrase when review finds a new opener of that kind.",
    "phrases": ["now", "no longer", "previously", "was changed", "added", "fixed", "used to", "as of", "new:", "changed", "removed", "renamed", "moved"],
    "directives": ["!", "type:", "noqa", "pragma", "fmt:"]
  },
  "practices": {
    "_comment": "Domain coding-practice profiles are DEFINED in config/practices.json (ai/cuda/langgraph) and ENABLED here — flip a flag to true to turn its domain checks on (e.g. cuda -> check_L naked-tensor, resource-context-manager advisory). All off keeps the template domain-neutral. Validated by check_structure.py (check_H); consumed by check_L and check_practices.py.",
    "profiles": {
      "ai": {{ "true" if "ai" in profiles else "false" }},
      "cuda": {{ "true" if "cuda" in profiles else "false" }},
      "langgraph": {{ "true" if "langgraph" in profiles else "false" }}
    }
  }
}
